WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.438
Master Prompts.

2
00:00:01.718 --> 00:00:04.214
From the Start Here part of the Soft Skill Playbook.

3
00:00:05.964 --> 00:00:06.988
The key idea.

4
00:00:07.268 --> 00:00:08.143
Start here.

5
00:00:08.423 --> 00:00:14.588
Paste one of these prompts into any AI assistant (Claude, ChatGPT, Gemini, etcetera.).

6
00:00:14.868 --> 00:00:20.628
Prompt 1 runs the whole day-by-day course with you: it teaches, role-plays, quizzes and corrects.

7
00:00:20.908 --> 00:00:29.036
The others generate conversations for any situation, train your sentences and questions, and coach you for real work conversations.

8
00:00:30.386 --> 00:00:31.730
Here is a quick table:

9
00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.461
Prompt, and Use it when.

10
00:00:33.741 --> 00:00:34.381
1.

11
00:00:34.661 --> 00:00:35.621
Course tutor:

12
00:00:35.901 --> 00:00:36.797
Every day.

13
00:00:37.077 --> 00:00:40.490
Type Start Day 1, then Start Day 2 tomorrow, and so on.

14
00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:41.474
2.

15
00:00:41.754 --> 00:00:43.311
Conversation generator:

16
00:00:43.591 --> 00:00:47.303
You need a conversation for a situation the course doesn't cover.

17
00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:48.330
3.

18
00:00:48.610 --> 00:00:50.487
Sentence and question trainer:

19
00:00:50.767 --> 00:00:55.311
You want extra grammar practice: making sentences, questions and answers.

20
00:00:55.591 --> 00:00:56.295
4.

21
00:00:56.575 --> 00:00:57.898
Communication coach:

22
00:00:58.178 --> 00:01:03.106
You're preparing for a real conversation: manager, CEO, interview, client.

23
00:01:04.456 --> 00:01:05.309
Part 1.

24
00:01:05.589 --> 00:01:07.381
Course tutor (use every day).

25
00:01:08.631 --> 00:01:10.509
Here is a template you can copy.

26
00:01:10.789 --> 00:01:12.943
You are my English and soft-skills tutor.

27
00:01:13.223 --> 00:01:18.749
You will run a day-by-day course with me. one lesson. per day, using the curriculum below.

28
00:01:19.029 --> 00:01:24.362
Be warm, patient and practical, like a good. teacher on a. one-to-one video call.

29
00:01:24.642 --> 00:01:45.101
ABOUT ME. - Name: your name. - Work: for example, software developer, 15 years experience. - First language: for example, Hindi or Telugu or Tamil or Bengali. - English level: beginner or intermediate or advanced. - I live in: city, country. - My goal: for example, speak confidently at work and while travelling.

30
00:01:45.381 --> 00:01:47.450
The rest of the template is on the page.

31
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:50.940
Tip:

32
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:52.287
How to use it.

33
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:54.337
Do one lesson a day, in order.

34
00:01:54.617 --> 00:02:01.017
Read the day's page on this site first (10 minutes), then run the same day with the tutor (10 to 15 minutes).

35
00:02:01.297 --> 00:02:04.071
On busy days, just do the role-play and the quiz.

36
00:02:05.421 --> 00:02:06.295
Part 2.

37
00:02:06.575 --> 00:02:09.157
Conversation generator (any situation).

38
00:02:10.407 --> 00:02:12.284
Here is a template you can copy.

39
00:02:12.564 --> 00:02:25.620
Create an English conversation practice pack for this situation: for example, renewing my car. insurance. at the agent's office or buying a SIM card or asking for a seat change on a bus.

40
00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:28.780
My level: beginner or intermediate or advanced.

41
00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.239
Country or city: for example, Bengaluru, India.

42
00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:33.265
Give me:

43
00:02:33.545 --> 00:02:34.185
1.

44
00:02:34.465 --> 00:02:36.855
10 key words and phrases (with meaning).

45
00:02:37.135 --> 00:02:37.839
2.

46
00:02:38.119 --> 00:02:44.945
8 questions I might ASK in this situation, and 8 questions I might BE ASKED, each. with a good answer.

47
00:02:45.225 --> 00:02:45.972
3.

48
00:02:46.252 --> 00:02:55.489
Two realistic conversations (10 to 14 lines each): one where everything goes smoothly. and one where. there is a problem that I solve politely.

49
00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:57.839
The rest of the template is on the page.

50
00:03:00.689 --> 00:03:01.585
Part 3.

51
00:03:01.865 --> 00:03:03.742
Sentence and question trainer.

52
00:03:04.992 --> 00:03:06.869
Here is a template you can copy.

53
00:03:07.149 --> 00:03:08.771
Be my English grammar trainer.

54
00:03:09.051 --> 00:03:21.915
Focus only on: making sentences or yes-no questions /. wh- questions /. answering questions or past tense or future or polite requests or articles and. prepositions.

55
00:03:22.195 --> 00:03:41.672
Run drills like this, one at a time, and wait for my answer: - Give me a statement; I turn it into a yes or no question, then a wh- question. - Give me a question; I answer it in a short answer and a full sentence. - Give me 3 jumbled words; I make a correct sentence. - Give me a sentence with a mistake; I fix it.

56
00:03:41.952 --> 00:03:48.075
After every 5 drills, give me a score, list my mistakes as, then, and explain the rule. in one line.

57
00:03:48.355 --> 00:03:52.365
Use examples from everyday life in India and from software work.

58
00:03:52.645 --> 00:03:53.883
Start now with drill.

59
00:03:54.163 --> 00:03:56.232
The rest of the template is on the page.

60
00:03:59.082 --> 00:03:59.999
Part 4.

61
00:04:00.279 --> 00:04:02.989
Communication coach (role-play and feedback).

62
00:04:04.239 --> 00:04:07.012
Copy the whole block, fill in the brackets, and send it.

63
00:04:08.362 --> 00:04:10.239
Here is a template you can copy.

64
00:04:10.519 --> 00:04:13.783
You are my personal communication and soft-skills coach.

65
00:04:14.063 --> 00:04:16.815
Treat it like a sparring. partner, not a lecture.

66
00:04:17.095 --> 00:04:32.093
ABOUT ME. - Role: for example, Senior Software Developer, 15 years experience. - Level of comfort speaking: beginner or intermediate or confident but want polish. - First language: for example, Hindi; I communicate at work in:

67
00:04:32.373 --> 00:04:45.002
English. - What I find hard: for example, speaking up in meetings, talking to senior leadership. saying no, small talk. - Goal for this session: for example, prepare for a one-on-one where I ask for a promotion.

68
00:04:45.282 --> 00:04:46.605
HOW TO RUN THE SESSION.

69
00:04:46.885 --> 00:04:47.525
1.

70
00:04:47.805 --> 00:04:51.559
Ask me up to 3 short questions to understand the situation.

71
00:04:51.839 --> 00:04:52.991
Don't ask more than.

72
00:04:53.271 --> 00:04:55.341
The rest of the template is on the page.

73
00:04:58.191 --> 00:04:59.151
Part 5.

74
00:04:59.431 --> 00:05:00.903
Ready-made variations.

75
00:05:02.153 --> 00:05:04.947
Swap the Goal and role-play lines for any of these.

76
00:05:06.297 --> 00:05:07.193
Manager.

77
00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.351
Here is a template you can copy.

78
00:05:09.631 --> 00:05:17.908
Goal: tell my manager the feature will slip by 1 week because of an unplanned API. dependency. and propose two options.

79
00:05:18.188 --> 00:05:21.857
You play my manager, who is under pressure from their own. boss.

80
00:05:24.707 --> 00:05:25.497
CEO.

81
00:05:25.777 --> 00:05:27.654
Here is a template you can copy.

82
00:05:27.934 --> 00:05:37.790
Goal: explain yesterday's 40-minute checkout outage to the CEO in under 2 minutes, and. ask for. approval to spend 2 sprints on reliability work.

83
00:05:38.070 --> 00:05:43.339
You play a busy, non-technical CEO. who cares. about revenue and customers.

84
00:05:46.189 --> 00:05:46.957
Junior.

85
00:05:47.237 --> 00:05:49.115
Here is a template you can copy.

86
00:05:49.395 --> 00:05:56.349
Goal: give feedback to a junior developer whose PR has no tests and poor naming. without. demotivating them.

87
00:05:56.629 --> 00:06:00.491
You play the junior: a bit defensive, eager to prove themselves.

88
00:06:03.341 --> 00:06:04.151
Airport.

89
00:06:04.431 --> 00:06:06.309
Here is a template you can copy.

90
00:06:06.589 --> 00:06:16.743
Goal: practise international airport conversations: check-in with extra baggage, a. security. question, immigration questions, and a delayed connecting flight.

91
00:06:17.023 --> 00:06:20.053
You play each. officer or staff. member in turn.

92
00:06:20.333 --> 00:06:23.149
Speak naturally and quickly like real staff do.

93
00:06:25.999 --> 00:06:26.767
Train.

94
00:06:27.047 --> 00:06:28.924
Here is a template you can copy.

95
00:06:29.204 --> 00:06:39.807
Goal: practise train-station conversations: asking about a platform change, requesting. a seat. swap, talking to the ticket examiner about a waitlisted ticket.

96
00:06:40.087 --> 00:06:43.969
You play the station. staff. a co-passenger and the TTE.

97
00:06:46.819 --> 00:06:47.651
Interview.

98
00:06:47.931 --> 00:06:49.809
Here is a template you can copy.

99
00:06:50.089 --> 00:06:53.331
Goal: practise a senior engineer behavioural interview.

100
00:06:53.611 --> 00:07:00.886
Ask me 5 questions (conflict. failure. leadership, influence without authority, a hard technical decision).

101
00:07:01.166 --> 00:07:04.025
Use the STAR. method to. judge my answers.

102
00:07:06.875 --> 00:07:07.877
Small talk.

103
00:07:08.157 --> 00:07:10.035
Here is a template you can copy.

104
00:07:10.315 --> 00:07:17.952
Goal: practise 5 minutes of small talk at a tech conference with a stranger who turns. out to be. a VP of Engineering.

105
00:07:18.232 --> 00:07:22.733
Help me start, keep it going, and exit politely with a LinkedIn. connection.

106
00:07:25.583 --> 00:07:26.522
Part 6.

107
00:07:26.802 --> 00:07:28.445
Mini prompts for daily use.

108
00:07:29.695 --> 00:07:31.039
Here is a quick table:

109
00:07:31.319 --> 00:07:32.407
When, and Prompt.

110
00:07:32.687 --> 00:07:34.692
Before sending an important message:

111
00:07:34.972 --> 00:07:37.724
Rewrite this to be clear, polite and confident.

112
00:07:38.004 --> 00:07:39.412
Keep it under 80 words.

113
00:07:39.692 --> 00:07:41.612
Put the main point first: paste.

114
00:07:41.892 --> 00:07:42.895
Before a meeting:

115
00:07:43.175 --> 00:07:45.393
I have a meeting with person about topic.

116
00:07:45.673 --> 00:07:50.068
Give me a 3-point structure, 2 likely objections and how to answer each.

117
00:07:50.348 --> 00:07:51.927
After a bad conversation:

118
00:07:52.207 --> 00:07:53.785
Here's what happened: story.

119
00:07:54.065 --> 00:07:55.559
What could I have said instead?

120
00:07:55.839 --> 00:07:57.823
Give me 3 phrases for next time.

121
00:07:58.103 --> 00:07:59.681
Explaining tech to non-tech:

122
00:07:59.961 --> 00:08:07.897
Explain technical issue to a CEO or customer or sales team in 3 sentences using one everyday analogy.

123
00:08:08.177 --> 00:08:09.628
Building vocabulary:

124
00:08:09.908 --> 00:08:15.305
Give me 10 professional alternatives to phrase I overuse, with an example sentence each.

125
00:08:16.655 --> 00:08:17.807
Confidence check:

126
00:08:18.087 --> 00:08:19.303
Read my message.

127
00:08:19.583 --> 00:08:25.109
Highlight every word that sounds unsure or over-apologetic and suggest a stronger version.

128
00:08:26.459 --> 00:08:27.099
Tip:

129
00:08:27.379 --> 00:08:28.488
Make it a habit.

130
00:08:28.768 --> 00:08:31.499
Do one 10-minute role-play a day for 30 days.

131
00:08:31.779 --> 00:08:34.488
Save the Phrases to memorise in a notes file.

132
00:08:34.768 --> 00:08:36.901
That file becomes your own phrase bank.

133
00:08:37.181 --> 00:08:39.037
Start from this playbook's Phrase Bank.

134
00:08:40.387 --> 00:08:41.134
Warning:

135
00:08:41.414 --> 00:08:42.310
Privacy.

136
00:08:42.590 --> 00:08:49.011
Don't paste confidential code, customer data, or names of real colleagues into public AI tools.

137
00:08:49.291 --> 00:08:51.979
Anonymise first (Person A, Service X).

138
00:08:53.329 --> 00:08:54.865
That's the end of this lesson.

139
00:08:55.145 --> 00:08:59.796
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

140
00:09:00.076 --> 00:09:06.199
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

